The warning of vibrating unexpectedly is only issued by weapons
whenever the final enchantment is > 5 as a warning.  This only
happens for some weapons (all artifacts, all elven weapons, 
otherwise a 1/7 chance).  It does not damage the weapon.  You
may get this warning many times in a row (if you beat the 2/3
odds of it evaporating several times in a row).
  
Next look at how it picks the enchantment amount:
---
chwepon(sobj,
        sobj->cursed ? -1 :
        !uwep ? 1 :
        uwep->spe >= 9 ? (rn2(uwep->spe) == 0) :
        sobj->blessed ? rnd(3-uwep->spe/3) : 1);
---
For highly enchanted weapons (=>9), the increase may be 0, even with
a blessed scroll.  Even with an increase of 0, there is still a
2/3 chance of it evaporating.  Hence when trying to enchant a +9 
weapon, the is a 2/3 chance of it evaporating, a 6/27 chance of 
nothing happening (glows violently), and a 1/27 chance of it 
increasing by 1 (even with a blessed scroll).  The odds get worse 
after +9.

If the weapon is only +3 to +5, the best increase you can get out 
of a blessed scroll is 2.  After +6, you can only increase in 
increments of 1.

Quiz: How can you get your favorite weapon up to +7 without any
      chance of it evaporating?

Answer: Engrave it up to +5, and read a blessed scroll of enchant
        weapon.  If is only at +6, engrave one letter on the ground
        and it will go down to +5.  Then try again with a blessed 
        scroll to get it to +7.

As for the Tsurugi, I don't think it gets worse if you enchant it
more - I think that's only Magicbane, because the enchantment
interferes with its special damage.
